Setup (one-time)

Install VS Code extensions — Python (Microsoft) and Jupyter (Microsoft) from the Extensions panel (Ctrl+Shift+X)
Open the terminal in VS Code (Ctrl+`) — PowerShell is fine
Create the virtual environment — python wasn't on PATH, so the py launcher was the fix:
py -m venv rag-env
Activate it:
rag-env\Scripts\activate

Confirmed by the (rag-env) prefix appearing on the prompt. (One-time creation — on future sessions you only run this activate line.)

Install the packages — once activated, plain python works because the venv put its own python.exe first on PATH:
python -m pip install pandas openpyxl
Verify:
python -c "import pandas; print(pandas.__version__)"

→ printed 3.0.5 ✓

The test itself

Save the Excel file into the project folder as test.xlsx
Create parse_test.py in the same folder:
python
import pandas as pd
from pathlib import Path

file = Path(__file__).parent / "test.xlsx"
sheets = pd.read_excel(file, sheet_name=None)

for name, df in sheets.items():
    print(f"\n=== Sheet: {name} — {df.shape[0]} rows x {df.shape[1]} cols ===")
    print(df.head(10))
    print("\nColumn types:")
    print(df.dtypes)
Run it:
python parse_test.py
Hit PermissionError → resolved it (file locked by Excel being open / OneDrive sync — closing Excel or copying the file cleared the lock), reran, and got the sheet output.

Gotchas encountered along the way, for the record:

python not recognized → use py, or reinstall Python with "Add to PATH" checked
Don't re-run py -m venv rag-env while the venv is active — it can't overwrite its own running python.exe (and you never need to recreate it anyway)
OneDrive folders cause file-lock headaches with this workflow — a plain local folder like C:\dev\ avoids it
pandas 3.0 shows text columns as str dtype where older tutorials say object — same thing, new name

